{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "66",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old man presented with recent-onset dry cough and exertional dyspnea. He had no significant past medical history. Initial contrast-enhanced CT of the chest revealed a 12 cm mass in the anterior superior mediastinum and multiple bilateral pulmonary nodules suggestive of metastatic disease. A few weeks later, he developed neck edema, distended neck veins, and facial plethora. Repeat CT confirmed invasion of the superior vena cava above the azygos vein and thrombus extending into the left brachiocephalic trunk. He was treated with heparin and high-dose corticosteroids, with rapid symptomatic relief; stenting was deferred. Video-assisted thoracoscopy yielded a wedge resection of a pulmonary lesion and biopsy of the mediastinal mass. Histopathology showed a multinodular proliferation of small, round, monomorphic cells with clear cytoplasm. Immunohistochemistry demonstrated diffuse strong WT1 positivity and moderate, patchy CD99 positivity.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. CIC-rearranged sarcoma\nB. A broad malignant mass was considered on imaging\nC. Ewing sarcoma was considered given the small round cell morphology and CD99 positivity but the pattern was atypical\nD. BCOR\nE. Mesenchymal chondrosarcoma was considered among small round cell tumors but excluded molecularly\nF. Poorly differentiated synovial sarcoma was listed among mimics but lacked the characteristic translocation\nG. Desmoplastic small round cell tumour was a histological mimic but no EWSR1\nH. Small cell osteosarcoma was considered but molecular testing showed no osteosarcoma\n",
